#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/README.md:24
msgid "Three habits"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/README.md:26
msgid "**Read the sentence.** It names the object and the rule. The explanation under it (or in _Explain_) names the alternative."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/README.md:28
msgid "**Look for a fix.** The inspector's **Fixes** section offers the actions the tool can take for a finding — _Choose what Temperature is represented by_, _Connect a driver to light_, _Detach … from …_ — as ordinary, undoable edits."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/README.md:31
msgid "**Orange is not red.** Orange and dashed mean _not decided yet_; red means _wrong now_. Only red stops anything."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:1
msgid "Connections"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:3
msgid "Links on the canvas mean one of three things — _this relationship reads that concept_, _this value drives that output_, _this port supplies that port_ — and each has rules. Some rules are enforced while you drag (the link will not land); others are recorded and then reported, because the tool would rather hold your intent and name the problem than refuse."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:9
msgid "The link will not land (forbidden cursor, no halo)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:11
msgid "**What it means.** The two sockets cannot be joined: they carry different concepts, or they are the same side (two inputs), or the same node — or you are dragging onto a collapsed behavior's socket, which is a picture, not a port."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:15
msgid "**Why.** Socket colour is the concept. A relationship reads a concept; it cannot read _Opacity_ through a socket meant for _Brightness_, whatever the numbers. For a behavior box, the link resolves to the member the socket stands for; a small chooser appears if several qualify."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:20
msgid "**What to do.** Drop on a socket of the same colour. If the concepts really should be one, that is a design change: make the relationship read the other concept, or bind the concept to the one you meant. In a system, a private concept of an instance is a different concept from the system's; share it in the component if it should be the same."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:26
msgid "_light expects Brightness, but dimByTilt produces Tilt → Brightness._ (under _Drives_ / _Driver_)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:28
msgid "**What it means.** The connected relationship does not produce exactly what the output accepts — a rule with inputs was connected, or a value of another concept."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:32
msgid "**Why.** A connection to an output converts nothing. Only a _value_ — a relationship without inputs — can be _the_ brightness at a tick."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:35
msgid "**What to do.** Connect the value that applies the rule (`brightness = dimByTilt(tilt)`), or a value of the right concept; disconnect the wrong one with _disconnect_ in the output's _Driver_ section. Code: `output.type_mismatch`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:40
msgid "_light updates in the interaction domain, but its driver shown updates in display._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:42
msgid "**Why.** A connection transports nothing. The device refreshes at its domain's rhythm and must read a value of that rhythm."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:45
msgid "**What to do.** Bring the value into the output's domain first — a value in _interaction_ that reads the other through `sync` — then connect that. Or set the output's _Updates in_ to the driver's domain, if that is what the product should do. Code: `output.clock_mismatch`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:50
msgid "The sink says _contested_; _light already has a final target._; _brightness, indicator all connect to light._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:52
msgid "**What it means.** Two or more values drive one output."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:54
msgid "**Why.** One output, one driver. Two values for one light is a contradiction, and BDL does not resolve it with a priority or a last writer."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:57
msgid "**What to do.** _Fixes_ offers _Detach … from …_ for each claimant and _Create upstream combination mapping_. Combine the competing values in one relationship — `if held then a else b` — and connect that. Code: `output.multiple_drivers`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:61
msgid "_A rule cannot drive an output — connect the value that applies this rule._ (under _Drives_)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:63
msgid "**What it means.** Not a finding: the selected relationship reads something, so its _Drives_ section offers no output. The same rule as above, stated at the source before anything is connected."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:67
msgid "**What to do.** When exactly one value of the design applies the rule, the section names it — _Show_ selects it; connect the output from there. Otherwise write the value (`brightness = dimByTilt(tilt)`) and connect that. An output's _Connect_ pop-up likewise lists only values of the concept it accepts; when there is none, it says so in one sentence."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:73
msgid "_Replace the connection?_ (a sheet)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:75
msgid "**What it means.** You dropped a link on a required port or a bound relationship that already has a source."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:78
msgid "**What to do.** _Disconnect and Connect_ to swap; _Cancel_ to keep the existing one. Nothing is ever swapped silently."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:81
msgid "_Carry across timing domains_ (a sheet asking _Starts at_)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:83
msgid "**What it means.** The two ends of the binding update in different domains."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:85
msgid "**What to do.** Enter the value the destination should see before the source has ever produced one, in the destination's units, and confirm — the binding carries the value across. Or cancel and give the destination the source's domain. See [Carrying a value across timing domains](../workflows/cross-domain-transport.md). Code when reported instead of asked: `system.binding_needs_transport`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:91
msgid "_second.tiltValue expects Tilt, but lampA.brightness provides Brightness._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:93
msgid "**Why.** A binding converts nothing: the provided port must promise exactly what the required port expects — same concept, input for input."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:96
msgid "**What to do.** Bind a port or value of the right concept. Code: `system.binding_type_mismatch`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:99
msgid "_Takes its value from adaptiveLamp.brightness_ and the formula field is gone"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:101
msgid "**What it means.** The relationship is bound to a port; the binding _is_ its definition."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:104
msgid "**What to do.** If you want to write its formula yourself, _Disconnect_ the binding first (the inspector says so)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:107
msgid "A banner: _concept Tilt is still used by 2 mapping(s)_ / _timing domain … is still used …_ / _output … is still driven by …_"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:109
msgid "**What it means.** You tried to delete something other objects depend on."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:111
msgid "**What to do.** Disconnect or delete the users first; the inspector names them under the delete button."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:114
msgid "A banner about a port in use, an instance in use, or a component that cannot stand in"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:116
msgid "Banner code"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:116
msgid "What to do"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:118
msgid "`system_edit.port_in_use`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:118
msgid "the port you tried to retire is bound on an instance"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:118
msgid "disconnect those bindings first"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:119
msgid "`system_edit.instance_in_use`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:119
msgid "the instance you tried to delete has bound ports"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:119
msgid "_Disconnect its ports first; the component stays._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:120
msgid "`system_edit.not_substitutable`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:120
msgid "_Replace with_ — the instance uses a port the other component lacks or promises differently"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:120
msgid "compare the two components' _Ports_; restore the port or choose another component"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:121
msgid "`group_edit.already_grouped`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:121
msgid "the relationship is already in another behavior"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:121
msgid "remove it there first"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:123
msgid "The component's name on an instance carries a red mark; _AdaptiveLamp's port brightness promises to update in main, but brightness updates in aux._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:125
msgid "**What it means.** The component's source no longer keeps its promise: a port's relationship has a different domain, is missing, or a required port has been given a definition."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:129
msgid "**Why.** A consumer bound to the port relies on what the port advertises."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:131
msgid "**What to do.** Fix the source (open it with _Edit Source_), or change the port's contract to match. Codes: `component.port_clock_mismatch`, `component.port_declaration_missing`, `component.required_port_realized`, `component.port_signature_mismatch`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/connection-errors.md:138
msgid "[Physical outputs](../concepts/physical-outputs.md) · [Behavior systems](../concepts/behavior-systems.md) · [System projects](../studio/system-projects.md)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/deployment-errors.md:1
msgid "Deployment"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/deployment-errors.md:3
msgid "Verdicts on the Deploy page are about **this design on this board**. They never change the design, and choosing another board never changes a Design-page verdict. The rules are in [Deploy](../studio/deploy.md)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/deployment-errors.md:7
msgid "_Fits Arduino Nano so far — the binding is not finished._ with _No device on arduino_nano for: light._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/deployment-errors.md:9
msgid "**What it means.** An output has a domain but no device realises it on the board."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/deployment-errors.md:12
msgid "**What to do.** _Add device_, choose a kind that can carry the output (a PWM channel for a dimmable light, a digital output for a switched load) and choose the output in the row. Code: `deploy.output_unrealised` (information)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/deployment-errors.md:16
msgid "_… so far …_ with _Not connected to an output: pwmLight._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/deployment-errors.md:18
msgid "**What it means.** A device has no output."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/deployment-errors.md:20
msgid "**What to do.** Choose one in the device row, or _Remove_ the device. A sensor that feeds the design rather than realising an output may stay unconnected. Code: `deploy.device_unbound` (information)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/deployment-errors.md:24
msgid "_Not feasible on Arduino Nano._ — _Could not place pwmLight PWM of pwmLight._ — _Nothing on arduino_nano can carry pwmLight PWM._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/deployment-errors.md:26
msgid "**What it means.** No pin on the board offers what one requirement needs (PWM, an interrupt, an I²C bus…), or every pin that does is already taken by another device — the page lists the blocking pins and who holds each."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/deployment-errors.md:30
msgid "**Why.** Placement is exact: each requirement needs a distinct resource with the right capability, on the same unit where the kind demands it (I²C SDA and SCL on one bus; a UART's TX and RX on one unit)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/deployment-errors.md:34
msgid "**What to do.** Fewer devices of that kind, a kind with lighter needs, a pin fixed by hand to free a contested one, or another board. The Nano has six PWM pins; _Big board (mock)_ has twelve. Code: `deploy.infeasible`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/deployment-errors.md:38
msgid "_Not feasible …_ — _The pin chosen by hand, D4, cannot carry pwmLight PWM here._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/deployment-errors.md:40
msgid "**What it means.** The pin you typed in the device row is not on this board or lacks the capability."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/deployment-errors.md:43
msgid "**What to do.** Clear the field to let the placement choose, or type a pin that has the capability (the placement table shows what each placed pin can do). Code: `deploy.infeasible`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/deployment-errors.md:47
msgid "The verdict is feasible but the status line still says _not causal_ or _outputs incomplete_"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/deployment-errors.md:49
msgid "**What it means.** The board fits the devices, but the design itself is not ready — a formula does not check, a cycle exists, or a required output is undriven. The Deploy page does not restate design problems."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/deployment-errors.md:53
msgid "**What to do.** Fix them on the Design page; the Deploy verdict is unaffected either way."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/deployment-errors.md:56
msgid "_Could not analyse: …_"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/deployment-errors.md:58
msgid "**What it means.** The request itself failed — the compiler service is not connected, or the project moved on while the analysis ran. Choose the board again, or check the status line's connection."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/deployment-errors.md:62
msgid "The dead end names a conflict that is not the real one"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/deployment-errors.md:64
msgid "**What it means.** _Not feasible_ reports the **first** dead end the placement hit, under its own order. It is one true conflict, not necessarily the one you would have named."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/deployment-errors.md:68
msgid "**What to do.** Resolve it and check again; if a second one appears, it was there too."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/deployment-errors.md:73
msgid "[Deploy](../studio/deploy.md) · [Physical outputs](../concepts/physical-outputs.md)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:1
msgid "Incomplete design"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:3
msgid "None of the situations on this page is an error. Each is a piece of the design that is not decided yet; the tool tells you which, and works with everything else. The idea behind this is in [Incomplete designs](../concepts/incomplete-designs.md)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:8
msgid "A dashed node with the word _declared_; the status line says _N not yet defined_"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:10
msgid "**What it is.** A relationship that reads something (or backs a port of the component you are editing) and has no formula yet. A relationship that reads nothing and has no formula is a Source — drawn with a bar at its left edge, not dashed, and counted as _N sources_, not as not yet defined: nothing is missing from it."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:16
msgid "**What to do.** Select it and write the formula in _Relationship_. If it was meant to be an input from outside, remove its reads instead: it becomes a Source."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:20
msgid "_tilt needs a value before simulation can step._ (Simulate)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:22
msgid "**What it is.** An input has no value for the run."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:24
msgid "**What to do.** Type one in its control on the left. Step is enabled once every input has a value."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:27
msgid "_Tilt needs a value form (Quantity, On / off or Count) before tilt can be given a value._ (Simulate)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:29
msgid "**What it is.** The concept an input produces is still _decide later_, so there is no control that could take a value."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:32
msgid "**What to do.** Select the concept (the _Show_ link) and choose its _Value_. Or use the fix _Choose what Tilt is represented by_."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:35
msgid "_Checked once Temperature's value is decided._ / _Temperature has no representation yet, so this formula cannot be checked._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:37
msgid "**What it is.** The formula reads a concept whose value form is not chosen. The relationship is _open_: not wrong, waiting. Its node is solid with a hollow socket."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:41
msgid "**What to do.** Decide the concept's value form. The formula is checked at once; nothing needs retyping. Code: `semantic.unbound_representation` (information)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:44
msgid "_dimByTilt has no definition. A relationship that reads something needs one before the design can run._ (Simulate)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:46
msgid "**What it is.** A rule without a formula is applied by something the simulator needs."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:49
msgid "**What to do.** Write the formula. (A _value_ without a formula is an input and is fine; a _rule_ without one has nothing to compute.)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:52
msgid "_level has no valid definition._ (Simulate)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:54
msgid "**What it is.** A relationship the run needs has a formula that does not check. This one _is_ an error — see [Types, units and concepts](type-and-concept-errors.md)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:58
msgid "_light has no final target yet._ / _Undriven — the design is incomplete without a driver._ / status line _outputs incomplete_"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:60
msgid "**What it is.** A required output with no driver. The design is incomplete, not wrong."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:63
msgid "**What to do.** Connect the value that should drive it: drag from its output socket to the sink, pick it in the output's _Connect_ pop-up, or take the fix _Connect a driver to light_. If the output is not essential, switch _Required_ off. Code: `output.missing_driver` (information)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:68
msgid "_light has no timing domain yet, so this connection cannot be checked._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:70
msgid "**What it is.** The output is _open_ — it has no domain, so nothing can be checked against it."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:73
msgid "**What to do.** Set _Updates in_ on the output. Code: `output.clock_unset` (information)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:76
msgid "_Open: nothing supplies it yet._ (an instance's port)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:78
msgid "**What it is.** A required port with no binding. The instance works with what it has; the simulator asks for the port's value as an input."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:81
msgid "**What to do.** Draw a link from a provided port or a top-level value to it — or leave it, if the value really comes from outside."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:84
msgid "_Fits Arduino Nano so far — the binding is not finished._ (Deploy)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:86
msgid "**What it is.** Every device that is bound fits, but an output has no device or a device no output."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:89
msgid "**What to do.** _Add device_ and choose the output; or choose an output in the device row. See [Deployment](deployment-errors.md)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/incomplete-design.md:94
msgid "[Incomplete designs](../concepts/incomplete-designs.md) · [Status meanings](../reference/status-meanings.md)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:1
msgid "Source files"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:3
msgid "Findings about a project's `.bdl` files, in Studio's Code view (under the editor), in a code editor (on the item) and in `bdld check` (with `file:line:col`). These findings are red: the file says something the design cannot mean. In a code editor the item is left out until it is fixed; in Studio's Code view the whole file waits (below). Everything else — a wrong unit, a missing driver, a domain crossing — is the same finding as on the canvas, on the same page of this section."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:11
msgid "A banner in the Code view: _This file does not build yet: the design shows the last version that did._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:13
msgid "What you typed does not read as a design yet — a syntax slip, or one of the findings below. Nothing is lost on either side: the canvas keeps showing the last version of the file that did build (its banner says _Showing the last version that built; the text has changes that do not build yet_), and the editor keeps your text exactly as typed. The reasons are listed under the editor; click one to put the cursor on it. Fix the text and the banners go as soon as the file builds again ([Design, Code and Split](../studio/code-view.md))."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:21
msgid "_A name is one word, without spaces: `Light_Output`._ · _`…` is a word of the language; choose another name._ · _A name is letters, digits and underscores, starting with a letter: `…`._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:23
msgid "A name on the canvas, in a sheet or in the inspector must be a name the source files can spell: letters, digits and underscores, not starting with a digit, not a keyword of the language, because the files _are_ the design. The message shows the spelling that would work. Projects saved by older versions of Studio that had such names get them respelled this way the first time they open. — `edit.invalid_name`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:30
msgid "_… is not a concept of this project; declare it with `concept … : …` or pick an existing one._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:32
msgid "The name in a signature, `output`, `use concept` or port is not declared in any file of the project. Declare it (any file under `src/`) or fix the spelling. Inside a component, only the body's own concepts and the ones it `use`s are in scope. — `text.unknown_concept`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:37
msgid "_… is not a timing domain here; declare it with `clock …`._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:39
msgid "The `@name` names no domain in scope: at the top level, a `clock` of the project; in a component, one of its `clock` or `param clock` items. — `text.unknown_clock`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:43
msgid "_… is not a relationship of this project._ · _… is not a physical output of this project._ · _… is not a component of this project._ · _… is not an instance of this project._ · _… has no port …_"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:45
msgid "A `drive`, `device … for`, `instance … : C`, `bind` or `export` names something that does not exist at that level. Check the spelling and where it is declared; a component's ports are the `requires`, `provides` and `param` items of its body. — `text.unknown_relationship`, `text.unknown_output`, `text.unknown_component`, `text.unknown_instance`, `text.unknown_port`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:51
msgid "_… is declared twice (… first); the first declaration is the one used._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:53
msgid "Two items of the same kind and name in the project (or in one component). The second is ignored. Rename or remove one. — `text.duplicate_item`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:56
msgid "_this output is already driven by …; an output has one driver, so this `drive` is ignored._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:58
msgid "Two `drive` lines for one output. An output has exactly one final driver ([Physical outputs](../concepts/physical-outputs.md)); combine the two relationships into one that drives it. — `text.second_driver`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:62
msgid "_… is a required port: what it needs comes from outside, so it has no definition here._ · _… is a parameter: each instance gives it a value, so it has no definition here._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:64
msgid "A `requires` or `param` port has a `name() = …` definition. Only a `provides` port is computed by the component; make it `provides`, or drop the definition. — `text.port_definition`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:68
msgid "_… is a timing parameter of …; give it a timing domain of the system._ · _… is neither a timing parameter nor a parameter port of …_"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:70
msgid "The braces of an instance give every `param clock` a domain of the system and every `param` a constant, by name. — `text.bad_argument`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:73
msgid "_a binding joins an instance's port to something; two top-level relationships are related by a formula, not a binding._ · _only an instance's port can be exported, as `instance.port`._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:75
msgid "`bind` needs an `instance.port` on at least one side; `export` takes exactly one. Two top-level relationships are connected by naming one in the other's formula. — `text.bad_binding`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:79
msgid "_… is not a value form; write Bool, Count or a quantity such as …_ · _… is not a device kind; one of pwm_channel, …_"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:81
msgid "The type after `concept … :` or the kind after `device … :` is not one the tool knows. — `text.unknown_representation`, `text.unknown_kind`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:84
msgid "_a parameter is a plain name (or `_`); patterns cannot destructure an input._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:86
msgid "`f(Some(x)) = …` is not allowed; name the input and `match` on it in the body. — `text.bad_parameter`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:89
msgid "_enums are syntax only in this version; the design model has no sum types yet._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:91
msgid "Reported as _open_, not an error: the `enum` parses and is skipped. — `text.unsupported_item`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:94
msgid "_… is new and more than one declaration of its kind disappeared from this file, so it could not be matched to an existing identity; it has a fresh one. Use the editor's rename to keep an identity._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:96
msgid "You renamed two items of the same kind in one file by hand between two reads of the project. The tools cannot tell which old item became which new one, so both are new: nothing semantic changes, but their canvas positions and group membership are gone. Rename one at a time, or use the editor's rename, which knows the identity ([Overview — Identity](../textual/overview.md#identity)). — `text.ambiguous_identity`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:103
msgid "A banner in Studio: _… changed on disk since the project was opened: …_"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:105
msgid "You pressed Save while a source file had been changed by another tool. Nothing was written. _Reload from disk_ takes the files as they are now (Studio's unsaved edits are dropped); _Overwrite_ writes Studio's version over them; _Dismiss_ leaves both as they are ([Authoring a project as text](../workflows/authoring-as-text.md)). — `project.changed_on_disk`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:112
msgid "On opening an older project: _a Flat project that already has files under src/: which is the design?_"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:114
msgid "The folder has both an older `design/*.json` design file and `.bdl` files under `src/`. Studio converts older projects to `.bdl` text the first time they open, but it will not guess which of the two is the design. Move one of them out of the folder and open again ([Project files — Older projects](../reference/project-files.md#older-projects)). — `project.text`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/text-project-errors.md:123
msgid "[Design, Code and Split](../studio/code-view.md) · [Syntax basics](../textual/syntax-basics.md) · [Types, units and concepts](type-and-concept-errors.md) · [Connections](connection-errors.md)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/timing-errors.md:3
msgid "Findings about _when_ values exist: under a relationship's **Timing** section, in the status line, and on the Simulate page's readiness list. The ideas are in [Timing](../concepts/timing.md)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/timing-errors.md:7
msgid "_These relationships depend on each other in the same instant: a, b. One of them must read the previous value instead._ (Simulate) / _a depends on its own current value._ (inspector) / status line _not causal_"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/timing-errors.md:9
msgid "**What it means.** Two or more values need each other within one tick — `a` uses `b` and `b` uses `a`, or a value uses itself — so there is no order in which they could be computed."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/timing-errors.md:13
msgid "**Why.** Within a tick every value is computed once from the current inputs. A cycle has no first value."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/timing-errors.md:16
msgid "**What to do.** Decide which of them should use _last tick's_ value and write it with `delay`: `a = delay(0, b + 1)`. A cycle through a `delay` is not instantaneous. If the cycle is a mistake, break it by changing what one of the formulas reads. Code: `reactive.instantaneous_cycle`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/timing-errors.md:21
msgid "_shown updates in a different timing domain from brightness. Choose how this relationship should observe the source value._ / status line _reads across domains_"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/timing-errors.md:23
msgid "**What it means.** A value's formula names a value from another timing domain directly."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/timing-errors.md:26
msgid "**Why.** Two domains share no instant; \"the current brightness\" has no meaning in the display's tick."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/timing-errors.md:29
msgid "**What to do.** Either read it through `sync(interaction, 0, brightness)` — the last value the source produced strictly before this tick, with a stated value to use before the source has ever run — or move the reader into the source's domain (_Updates in_) and accept its timing. The _Insert explicit sync_ fix is listed but blocked today; write the `sync` yourself. Code: `clock.cross_domain_reference`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/timing-errors.md:36
msgid "_acc remembers a value over time but has no timing domain._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/timing-errors.md:38
msgid "**What it means.** A formula uses `delay` or `sync` but its relationship is on _Any timing domain_."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/timing-errors.md:41
msgid "**Why.** Remembering means reading a domain at its previous activation; without a domain there is no \"previous\"."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/timing-errors.md:44
msgid "**What to do.** Set _Updates in_ on the relationship. Code: `clock.temporal_without_domain`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/timing-errors.md:47
msgid "_`delay` can only be used in a relationship without inputs._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/timing-errors.md:49
msgid "**What it means.** `delay` or `sync` was written inside a rule (a relationship with inputs)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/timing-errors.md:52
msgid "**Why.** Memory belongs to a value as a whole — one value per activation — not to a formula over its inputs."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/timing-errors.md:55
msgid "**What to do.** Read the inputs through values (relationships without inputs) and remember there: make the rule pure, and put the `delay` in the value that applies it. Code: `formula.temporal.under_inputs`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/timing-errors.md:59
msgid "_`delay` cannot be used inside a block's result or a match arm._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/timing-errors.md:61
msgid "**What to do.** Bind it first — `let previous = delay(0, x);` — or make it the value the `match` looks at. Code: `formula.temporal.under_binder`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/timing-errors.md:64
msgid "_`fast` is not a timing domain of this design._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/timing-errors.md:66
msgid "**What to do.** The first argument of `sync` is the _source's_ domain, spelled as in the sidebar's _Timing domains_; create it first if it does not exist. Code: `formula.sync.unknown_domain`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/timing-errors.md:70
msgid "The trace has an empty cell where you expected a value"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/timing-errors.md:72
msgid "**What it means.** The value's domain did not activate at that tick (the _active_ column names the ones that did). Not an error."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/timing-errors.md:75
msgid "**What to do.** Nothing, or change that domain's period on the Simulate page."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/timing-errors.md:77
msgid "An instance's _Timing_ says a domain is missing — _second does not say which timing domain main is._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/timing-errors.md:79
msgid "**What to do.** Choose a system domain for each of the instance's timing parameters in its inspector. Code: `system.clock_argument_missing`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/timing-errors.md:84
msgid "[Timing](../concepts/timing.md) · [Carrying a value across timing domains](../workflows/cross-domain-transport.md)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:1
msgid "Types, units and concepts"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:3
msgid "Findings under the formula field, in red. The formula was checked as you typed; the relationship becomes _invalid_ only if you save it as it is. The rules behind these are in [Concepts](../concepts/concepts.md) and [Relationships](../concepts/relationships.md)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:8
msgid "_Brightness is a dimensionless quantity, but this formula produces an angle._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:10
msgid "**What it means.** The signature promises one concept; the formula's result has a different value form or dimension. Here a _Tilt_ was returned where a _Brightness_ (a plain number) was expected."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:14
msgid "**Why.** A relationship produces exactly the concept it promises. The result is not scaled or reinterpreted."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:17
msgid "**What to do.** Make the arithmetic come out in the promised dimension — `Tilt / 90 deg` divides an angle by an angle — or change _Produces_ if the relationship really produces something else. Code: `realization.type_mismatch`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:21
msgid "_This expression adds values with different physical dimensions: an angle and a time._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:23
msgid "**What it means.** Two operands of `+`, `-`, a comparison, or `if` branches do not have the same dimension."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:26
msgid "**Why.** An angle plus a time has no meaning; BDL checks dimensions like a physicist does."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:29
msgid "**What to do.** Usually a unit is missing on a number: `Tilt > 45` should be `Tilt > 45 deg`. Multiplication and division combine dimensions freely; addition, subtraction and comparison need the same one. Code: `dimension.mismatch`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:34
msgid "_`foobar` is not a unit._ — _Units available: rad deg s ms min h m mm cm km kg g A mA K cd mol Hz N Pa kPa W V mV lx._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:36
msgid "**What to do.** Use one of the listed units, or write the quantity without a unit if it really is a plain number. Code: `formula.unit.unknown`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:39
msgid "_`x` is not something this mapping reads or can call._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:41
msgid "**What it means.** A name in the formula is neither a concept this relationship reads nor a relationship of the design."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:44
msgid "**What to do.** Inside a **rule**, name the concepts it reads (_Tilt_, as in the _Reads_ section). Inside a **value**, name the design's values (`tilt`) and apply its rules (`dimByTilt(tilt)`). Check the spelling — completion (⌃Space) lists what is in scope. Code: `formula.name.unknown`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:49
msgid "_dimByTilt does not read AmbientLight._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:51
msgid "**What it means.** The formula names a concept the relationship does not read."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:53
msgid "**Why.** A rule sees only its inputs; that is what makes it a rule."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:55
msgid "**What to do.** Add the concept under _Reads_ (the fix _Connect AmbientLight to dimByTilt as an input_ does it), or drop it from the formula. Code: `formula.name.not_an_input`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:59
msgid "_dimByTilt reads Tilt here, but this is Brightness._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:61
msgid "**What it means.** You applied a rule to a value of the wrong concept."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:63
msgid "**Why.** Concepts are identities: a _Brightness_ is never a _Tilt_, even if both are plain numbers."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:66
msgid "**What to do.** Pass a Tilt — an input of this relationship, or a value that produces Tilt. Code: `formula.call.argument_type`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:69
msgid "_tilt reads nothing; it is a value, not something to apply._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:71
msgid "**What to do.** Write `tilt`, not `tilt()`. Code: `formula.call.arity`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:73
msgid "_dimByTilt reads Tilt; give it those values._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:75
msgid "**What it means.** A rule was named as if it were a value."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:77
msgid "**Why.** A rule with inputs can only be applied; it cannot be stored or passed along."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:80
msgid "**What to do.** Write `dimByTilt(tilt)`. Code: `formula.mapping.needs_arguments`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:83
msgid "_Only a named relationship can be applied._ / _Tilt is a concept, not a relationship; it cannot be applied._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:85
msgid "**What to do.** Calls apply a relationship of the design to its inputs; the result of a call cannot be called again (`f(x)(y)`), and a concept has nothing to apply. Code: `formula.call.not_a_relationship`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:89
msgid "A number pattern in `match` is refused — _This pattern is a plain number, but the value matched is a count._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:91
msgid "**What to do.** Counts cannot be matched against number patterns; write `if x == 10 then … else …`. Code: `formula.unsupported` / `dimension.mismatch`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:94
msgid "_… is not exhaustive_ / an arm is unreachable"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:96
msgid "**What to do.** Add the missing case (`_ =>` catches the rest), or remove the arm that can never match. Codes: `formula.match.non_exhaustive` (error), `formula.match.unreachable` (warning)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:100
msgid "A syntax error"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:102
msgid "**What it means.** The text does not parse; the finding points at the place and says what was expected (_comparisons cannot be chained — write `a < b && b < c`_, _a unit can only follow a number, as in `90 deg`_)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:106
msgid "**What to do.** Fix the text; the check re-runs as you type. Code: `formula.parse.unexpected_token`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/troubleshooting/type-and-concept-errors.md:111
msgid "[Formula language](../reference/formula-language.md) · [Formula editor](../studio/formula-editor.md)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/authoring-as-text.md:1
msgid "Authoring a project as text"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/authoring-as-text.md:3
msgid "**Goal:** write the tilt lamp as `.bdl` files, check it from your editor, open the same project in Studio, and keep both sides agreeing."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/authoring-as-text.md:6
msgid "**You need:** the workspace built (`cargo build --workspace`), an editor with `bdl-lsp` configured ([Editor and language server](../textual/editor-and-lsp.md)), Studio running."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/authoring-as-text.md:10
#: docs/user-guide/workflows/component-versioning.md:11
#: docs/user-guide/workflows/composing-components.md:8
#: docs/user-guide/workflows/grouping-behavior.md:12
#: docs/user-guide/workflows/multi-output-behavior.md:6
#: docs/user-guide/workflows/package-as-component.md:7
#: docs/user-guide/workflows/sensor-to-output.md:15
msgid "Steps"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/authoring-as-text.md:12
msgid "**Create the project.** In Studio's welcome page choose _New Project…_ and pick a folder. Or from a terminal make the folder by hand:"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/authoring-as-text.md:21
msgid "Studio shows an empty system canvas; on disk there is `src/main.bdl` with a comment, and the two tool-owned sidecars ([Project files](../reference/project-files.md))."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/authoring-as-text.md:25
msgid "**Write the design.** In your editor, `src/main.bdl`:"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/authoring-as-text.md:44
msgid "As you type, findings appear on the line they concern; hovering `Brightness` in the signature shows _concept Brightness : Scalar_. Split the vocabulary into `src/concepts.bdl` if you like — items may live in any file under `src/`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/authoring-as-text.md:49
msgid "**Save.** The editor writes the file; the server re-reads the project and writes `.bdl/identities.json`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/authoring-as-text.md:52
msgid "**Check from the command line** (optional):"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/authoring-as-text.md:58
msgid "prints `lamp: 2 concept(s), 3 relationship(s), 1 output(s); checks, outputs complete` — or each finding with its file and line and a non-zero exit ([Command line](../reference/cli.md))."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/authoring-as-text.md:63
msgid "**Open it in Studio.** _Open Project…_, choose the folder. The canvas shows the three relationships, the output and its driver, with the description on `dimByTilt`'s sheet. Simulate as usual ([First simulation](../getting-started/first-simulation.md))."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/authoring-as-text.md:68
msgid "**Edit on the canvas and save (⌘S).** Rename _Tilt_ to _Lean_ in the sheet, save. On disk, `main.bdl` now reads `concept Lean : Angle`, `mapping dimByTilt : Lean -> Brightness` — and everything else, comments included, is as you wrote it. The item kept its identity: its position on the canvas is where you left it."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/authoring-as-text.md:74
msgid "**Edit the text while Studio has the project open.** Add `concept Glow : Scalar` in your editor and save. Then in Studio make any edit and press ⌘S. Studio refuses with a banner — _1 changed on disk since the project was opened: src/main.bdl_ — and two buttons: **Reload from disk** takes the file's version (Studio's unsaved edits are dropped); **Overwrite** writes Studio's version over the file. Choose _Reload from disk_: _Glow_ appears on the canvas, every other item keeps its identity and place."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/authoring-as-text.md:82
#: docs/user-guide/workflows/component-versioning.md:32
#: docs/user-guide/workflows/composing-components.md:36
#: docs/user-guide/workflows/cross-domain-transport.md:53
#: docs/user-guide/workflows/grouping-behavior.md:51
