#: docs/user-guide/workflows/sensor-to-output.md:76
msgid "If it does not work"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/authoring-as-text.md:96
msgid "**Studio's banner says a file changed but you did not edit it.** A formatter or your editor's save touched it. _Reload from disk_ is safe when you have no unsaved canvas edits."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/authoring-as-text.md:99
msgid "**After retyping several names by hand, positions on the canvas were lost.** Two renames of the same kind in one file at once cannot be told apart; the items got fresh identities (a finding on each says so). Rename one at a time, or use the editor's rename."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/authoring-as-text.md:103
msgid "**An item is a finding in the editor but fine in Studio** (or the reverse): one side has unsaved changes. Save on both sides; the two read the same files."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/authoring-as-text.md:105
msgid "**`bdld check` exits 2 with _error:_** — the folder has no `bdl.toml`, or it is an older project that has both `src/` files and a `design/*.json` file; keep one of the two."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/authoring-as-text.md:111
msgid "[Syntax basics](../textual/syntax-basics.md) · [Composing components](composing-components.md) — the same design as components and instances, in text as `component` / `instance` / `bind`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/component-versioning.md:1
msgid "Versioning a component"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/component-versioning.md:3
msgid "**Goal.** Make a second version of _AdaptiveLamp_, move one instance to it, and see what the tool checks when an instance changes component — and what it does not."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/component-versioning.md:7
msgid "Editing a component's source changes **every** instance at once. When you want some instances to keep the old behavior, you make a **version**: a separate component with the same promise."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/component-versioning.md:13
msgid "**Duplicate.** Select _AdaptiveLamp_ in the sidebar → **Duplicate as Version**. Name it `AdaptiveLamp v2`. A second component appears with the same ports and the same source; it has no instances yet."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/component-versioning.md:16
msgid "**Change v2.** _Edit Source_ on v2; change `brightness` to `dimByTilt(tiltValue) / 2`; _Save definition_; _‹ System_."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/component-versioning.md:18
msgid "**Move an instance.** Select `second` → under **Implemented by**, **Replace with** _AdaptiveLamp v2_. The instance keeps its name, its place, and every binding; it now runs v2's source. `adaptiveLamp` still runs the original."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/component-versioning.md:21
msgid "**Simulate.** ⌘2, Step: `adaptiveLamp.brightness` and `second.brightness` now differ."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/component-versioning.md:23
msgid "**Retire a port on v2.** Select v2 → _Ports_ → **Retire port** on _tiltValue_. Refused with a banner: `second`'s _tiltValue_ is bound. A port in use cannot be retired. Disconnect `second`'s _tiltValue_ first, then retire it (the source's `tiltValue` relationship stays; it is just no longer promised)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/component-versioning.md:28
msgid "**Try to move the first instance to v2.** _Replace with_ v2 on `adaptiveLamp`. Refused: `adaptiveLamp` uses a port v2 no longer offers. Move it back, or restore the port."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/component-versioning.md:34
msgid "A version is a **copy with the same promise**, made by _Duplicate as Version_. Its ports carry the same identities, which is what lets an instance move between the two. A component authored separately, even with identical-looking ports, cannot stand in for another."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/component-versioning.md:38
msgid "**Replace with** is checked for **wiring**: every port the instance uses must exist on the new component with a compatible contract (same concept, same timing relation). If it does, the bindings survive; if not, the replacement is refused and nothing changes."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/component-versioning.md:42
msgid "It is **not** checked for **behavior**: nothing verifies that v2 computes the same values as v1. That is your decision — it is the point of a version — and the simulator is how you compare."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/component-versioning.md:45
msgid "A **contract change** on a port (say, from _a timing parameter_ to _any domain_) is an edit like any other: the tool re-checks whether the source keeps the new promise and marks the component and its instances when it does not. ⌘Z undoes it."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/component-versioning.md:52
msgid "_A bound port cannot be retired_ — disconnect the instances' bindings on that port first."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/component-versioning.md:54
msgid "_Replace with_ refused — the instance uses a port the target lacks, or a port whose contract differs; compare the two components' _Ports_."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/component-versioning.md:56
msgid "Deleting a component is refused — _Delete its instances first._ Deleting an instance is refused — _Disconnect its ports first; the component stays._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/component-versioning.md:61
msgid "[Components](../concepts/components.md) · [Behavior systems](../concepts/behavior-systems.md)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/composing-components.md:1
msgid "Composing components"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/composing-components.md:3
msgid "**Goal.** Place a second instance of _AdaptiveLamp_ from [Packaging a behavior](package-as-component.md), feed both from the same tilt, and use each one's brightness — then see what the tool does when two instances would drive one light."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/composing-components.md:10
msgid "**A second instance.** Right-click empty canvas → **Add Instance ▸ AdaptiveLamp**. Name it `second`. It appears with an open _tiltValue_ socket (hollow) on the left and _brightness_ on the right."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/composing-components.md:13
msgid "**Its timing.** Select it. Under **Timing**, the component's parameter _main_ needs a system domain: choose _main_. (An instance created from a packaging already has this set.)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/composing-components.md:16
msgid "**Fan out the tilt.** Drag from `tiltValue`'s output socket onto `second`'s _tiltValue_ socket. One top-level value now feeds two instances: the value is computed once and read by both."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/composing-components.md:19
msgid "**Use the second brightness.** _Mappings_ **+**: `mirror`, reads nothing, produces Brightness, updates in _main_, no formula. Drag from `second`'s _brightness_ socket onto `mirror`'s socket: `mirror` is now _bound to second.brightness_."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/composing-components.md:23
msgid "**Try to replace a source.** Drag from `adaptiveLamp`'s _brightness_ socket onto `mirror` as well. A sheet asks _Replace the connection?_ — **Disconnect and Connect** or **Cancel**. Cancel. A required port or a bound relationship has one source; the tool never swaps it silently."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/composing-components.md:27
msgid "**Simulate.** ⌘2. The inputs are the system's (`raw`); the trace has columns `adaptiveLamp.brightness`, `second.brightness`, `mirror`, `brightness`, `indicator`, and the light. Step: both instances compute the same values from the same tilt."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/composing-components.md:31
msgid "**Make the conflict.** On Design, connect `mirror` to the _light_ sink as well (drag onto the sink). The light is **contested** — two values, from two instances, one output — reported exactly as in a plain design. Detach one via _Fixes_."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/composing-components.md:38
msgid "An **instance** is one use of a component's source with fresh identities of its own: `second.brightness` and `adaptiveLamp.brightness` are two values, even though one source defines both. Here they are two values of the system's _Brightness_, because packaging shares the concepts the group used; a component authored from scratch keeps its concepts private — one per instance — unless you share them ([Components](../concepts/components.md))."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/composing-components.md:44
msgid "A **binding** is a definition by identity: `mirror` _is_ `second.brightness`. No conversion, no copying. **Fan-out** is the same value read from several places."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/composing-components.md:47
msgid "**One source per destination.** Rebinding is explicit: _Disconnect and Connect_."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/composing-components.md:49
msgid "**Outputs stay the system's.** Two instances cannot both drive the light; the rule is the single-driver rule you already know."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/composing-components.md:51
msgid "Everything is one design to the checker and simulator: an instance's values are simulated alongside the system's, and an instance's open port shows up as an input."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/composing-components.md:57
msgid "The link will not land — the concepts differ: the port carries the component's _private_ Brightness while your relationship produces the system's, or the reverse. Share the concept in the component, or bind to a relationship of the right concept. [Connections](../troubleshooting/connection-errors.md)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/composing-components.md:61
msgid "The sheet asks _Carry across timing domains_ — the two ends update in different domains; see [Carrying a value across timing domains](cross-domain-transport.md)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/composing-components.md:64
msgid "_Open: nothing supplies it yet._ on a port — expected until you bind it; the simulator will ask for it as an input meanwhile."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/composing-components.md:69
msgid "[Carrying a value across timing domains](cross-domain-transport.md) · [Versioning a component](component-versioning.md)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/cross-domain-transport.md:1
msgid "Carrying a value across timing domains"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/cross-domain-transport.md:3
msgid "**Goal.** Show the lamp's brightness on a slow display that refreshes on its own rhythm — and see why BDL asks for an initial value when a value crosses from one timing domain to another."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/cross-domain-transport.md:7
msgid "There are two places this happens, with one rule behind them: inside a **formula** (`sync`), and on a **binding** between an instance's port and something in another domain (_Starts at_)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/cross-domain-transport.md:11
msgid "In a formula"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/cross-domain-transport.md:13
msgid "**A second domain.** _Timing domains_ **+**: `display`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/cross-domain-transport.md:14
msgid "**A value in it.** _Mappings_ **+**: `shown`, reads nothing, produces Brightness, **Updates in** _display_."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/cross-domain-transport.md:16
msgid "**Read across.** Give `shown` the formula `brightness` and _Add definition_. The status line says **reads across domains**, and under the relationship's _Timing_ section: _shown updates in a different timing domain from brightness. Choose how this relationship should observe the source value._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/cross-domain-transport.md:20
msgid "**Carry it.** Change the formula to"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/cross-domain-transport.md:26
msgid "_Save definition._ The finding is gone. `shown` now reads the last value `brightness` had at _interaction_'s most recent activation strictly before `display`'s tick, and `0` until _interaction_ has run."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/cross-domain-transport.md:30
msgid "**Simulate.** ⌘2. Set _display_ to _every 3_. Step ×10: `shown` is filled only on ticks 0, 3, 6, 9; at tick 0 it reads `Brightness(0)` — the initial value — and afterwards the brightness from the last _interaction_ tick _before_ its own."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/cross-domain-transport.md:35
msgid "On a binding"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/cross-domain-transport.md:37
msgid "With the system from [Composing components](composing-components.md):"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/cross-domain-transport.md:39
msgid "**A value in another domain.** _Timing domains_ **+**: `aux`; _Mappings_ **+**: `slow`, reads nothing, produces Brightness, updates in _aux_, no formula."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/cross-domain-transport.md:42
msgid "**Bind across.** Drag from `adaptiveLamp`'s _brightness_ socket (domain _main_) onto `slow`. A sheet: _Carry across timing domains_ — from _main_ to _aux_ — with a **Starts at** field. Enter `0` and click **Connect**."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/cross-domain-transport.md:45
msgid "The link carries a gate mark; the binding's inspector says _Carried across timing domains: the destination sees the last value …_ with _Starts at 0_. Cancelling instead makes no binding: a transport is never inserted for you."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/cross-domain-transport.md:51
msgid "_Binding across timing domains asks for a starting value._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/cross-domain-transport.md:55
msgid "Two domains have **no shared instant**. \"The current brightness\" has no meaning in the display's tick; only \"the brightness at _interaction_'s last tick before now\" does. That is what `sync` and a transported binding read."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/cross-domain-transport.md:58
msgid "**Strictly before**: a source value produced at the same global tick is not visible yet. This is what makes the result independent of the order in which a device happens to process two domains that fire together."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/cross-domain-transport.md:61
msgid "The **initial value** is yours to choose, in the destination's units, because the destination may tick before the source ever has. BDL does not invent a zero."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/cross-domain-transport.md:64
msgid "A transport is a **meaning-changing decision**, so it is always written (in the formula) or confirmed (in the sheet); the tool offers no automatic sync."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/cross-domain-transport.md:69
msgid "_reads across domains_ stays — the formula still names a value from another domain outside `sync`; every such read needs its own `sync`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/cross-domain-transport.md:71
msgid "`sync` refused _under inputs_ / _under a binder_ — `sync` (like `delay`) belongs in a value's formula, at the top level or in a `let`, an `if` branch or a `match` scrutinee; not in a rule with inputs, a `match` arm or a block's result."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/cross-domain-transport.md:75
msgid "The domain name in `sync(...)` is unknown — it is the _source's_ domain, spelled as in the sidebar."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/cross-domain-transport.md:80
msgid "[Timing](../concepts/timing.md) · [Troubleshooting: timing](../troubleshooting/timing-errors.md)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/grouping-behavior.md:1
msgid "Grouping a behavior"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/grouping-behavior.md:3
msgid "**Goal.** Gather the lamp's dimming relationships into one named behavior, _Adaptive lamp_, and see the design from a step back — without changing what it does."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/grouping-behavior.md:7
msgid "**Needs** a project with a few relationships — the lamp of [Your first behavior](../getting-started/first-behavior.md) — or the system built in the [tests](../VERIFICATION.md): `raw` → `tiltValue` → `dimByTilt` → `brightness` → `light`, with `indicator = brightness`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/grouping-behavior.md:14
msgid "**Select the members.** ⇧-drag a box around `dimByTilt` and `brightness` on the canvas. The inspector shows _2 selected_ and **Group 2 relationships as Behavior**, with a note that they read each other."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/grouping-behavior.md:17
msgid "**Group.** Click it (or right-click → **Group as Behavior**). A region named _Behavior_ surrounds the two nodes and its name opens for editing: type `Adaptive lamp`, Return."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/grouping-behavior.md:20
msgid "**Look at the boundary.** Select the region. The **Boundary** section lists _Inputs: tiltValue_ (what the members read from outside), _Outputs: brightness_ (what outside reads), _Physical outputs: light_ (driven by a member), _Internal: dimByTilt_. You did not choose any of this; it is read off the dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/grouping-behavior.md:31
msgid "**Collapse.** Right-click the region → **Collapse**. The two nodes become one box with a socket _tiltValue_ on the left and _brightness_ on the right; the links that entered and left the members now enter and leave the box. Zoom out: below half size every behavior reads as its box anyway."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/grouping-behavior.md:38
msgid "_The same behavior collapsed: one box with an aggregate socket for the member each link reaches._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/grouping-behavior.md:41
msgid "**Notice what the box's sockets are.** Each is labelled with the member it stands for. A link dragged from or onto one resolves to that member — the box itself is never an end of a link."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/grouping-behavior.md:44
msgid "**Expand**, drag `indicator` into the region: it joins. Drag it out onto empty canvas: it leaves. Right-click `indicator` → **Add to Group ▸** does the same."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/grouping-behavior.md:47
msgid "**Check nothing changed.** ⌘2, Step a few ticks: the trace is the one you had, and a run that was already there is kept. A group edit is not a design change: the analysis is not redone and the status line does not move."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/grouping-behavior.md:53
msgid "A behavior is **authoring metadata**: a name and a membership list. The compiler never reads it. Every value, every verdict, the simulation and the placement are those of the ungrouped design."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/grouping-behavior.md:56
msgid "The box's sockets are the **boundary as computed**: crossing-in and crossing-out dependencies. They are a picture. A link cannot attach to them, so nothing can ever depend on \"the group\"."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/grouping-behavior.md:59
msgid "Undo (⌘Z) covers group edits, in one history with the design edits; undoing one changes no revision."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/grouping-behavior.md:64
msgid "A banner refuses adding a relationship — it is already in another behavior; a relationship belongs to at most one."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/grouping-behavior.md:66
msgid "The **Boundary** section says _Computed once the analysis arrives._ — wait a moment, or fix a formula the compiler could not check."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/grouping-behavior.md:71
msgid "[Packaging a behavior as a component](package-as-component.md)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/multi-output-behavior.md:1
msgid "A second output"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/multi-output-behavior.md:3
msgid "**Goal.** Give the lamp a status indicator that shows the same brightness as the light — and see what BDL does when two values compete for one output."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/multi-output-behavior.md:8
msgid "**A second output.** _Outputs_ **+**: `Indicator`, accepts **Brightness**, updates in _interaction_, _Required_ off (a missing indicator should not make the design incomplete)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/multi-output-behavior.md:11
msgid "**A value for it.** _Mappings_ **+**: `indicator`, reads nothing, produces Brightness; **Updates in** _interaction_; formula `brightness`. _Add definition._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/multi-output-behavior.md:15
msgid "A value's formula may simply name another value. `indicator` is `brightness`, read at the same tick."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/multi-output-behavior.md:18
msgid "**Connect.** Drag from `indicator`'s output socket onto the _Indicator_ sink."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/multi-output-behavior.md:19
msgid "**Now make a conflict.** Drag from `brightness`'s output socket onto the _Indicator_ sink too."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/multi-output-behavior.md:22
msgid "The sink shows the word **contested**. Select it: the _Driver_ section lists both claimants, and under _Fixes_ the tool offers _Detach brightness from Indicator_ / _Detach indicator from Indicator_ and _Create upstream combination mapping_. The status line says _outputs incomplete_."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/multi-output-behavior.md:27
msgid "**Resolve it.** Click _Detach brightness from Indicator_. The indicator is driven by `indicator` alone."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/multi-output-behavior.md:32
msgid "One physical output, **one driver**. Two values driving one light is a contradiction in the design; BDL names both claimants and offers to detach one or to combine the values upstream. It never picks a winner — there is no priority, no \"last one wins\"."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/multi-output-behavior.md:36
msgid "A **required** output that is undriven makes the design _incomplete_; an **optional** one does not."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/multi-output-behavior.md:38
msgid "Two outputs may show the same value (`brightness` drives the light, `indicator = brightness` drives the indicator) — the sharing happens in the formula, not at the output."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/multi-output-behavior.md:41
msgid "A contested output does not make its drivers _invalid_: `brightness` still checks. The finding is about the connection and sits under _Drives_ and _Driver_."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/multi-output-behavior.md:47
msgid "_A rule cannot drive an output — connect the value that applies this rule._ — the rule's _Drives_ section offers no output; connect the value it names (_Show_ selects it), or write one."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/multi-output-behavior.md:50
msgid "The connection is recorded but reported under _Driver_ — the value's domain differs from the output's, or it produces another concept. [Connections](../troubleshooting/connection-errors.md)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/multi-output-behavior.md:56
msgid "[Physical outputs](../concepts/physical-outputs.md) · [Grouping a behavior](grouping-behavior.md)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/package-as-component.md:1
msgid "Packaging a behavior as a component"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/package-as-component.md:3
msgid "**Goal.** Turn the _Adaptive lamp_ behavior from [Grouping a behavior](grouping-behavior.md) into a reusable component with a promise — and keep the design computing exactly what it did."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/package-as-component.md:9
msgid "**Open the sheet.** Right-click the _Adaptive lamp_ region → **Package as Reusable Component…** (or the inspector's button of the same name). The sheet reads _Working out the boundary…_ and then fills in."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/package-as-component.md:12
msgid "**Read what was computed.** **Requires: tiltValue** — the members read it from outside, so the component will need it. **Provides: brightness** — outside reads it, so the component will offer it. **Timing parameters: main** — the domain the members use. These are the floor: the sheet never offers to remove one."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/package-as-component.md:17
msgid "**Make the four decisions.**"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/package-as-component.md:18
msgid "Component name: `AdaptiveLamp`. The instance name follows (`adaptiveLamp`); change it if you like."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/package-as-component.md:20
msgid "_Open relationships_ — members without a formula, if any: **Treat as input** (a required port) or **Keep internal**. None here."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/package-as-component.md:22
msgid "_Physical outputs_ — `light`, driven by a member: **Stays the system's** (the component provides `brightness`, the system keeps the light) or **Moves inside** (the light becomes the component's). Keep it the system's."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/package-as-component.md:25
msgid "Every change re-asks the preview."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/package-as-component.md:26
msgid "**Package.** One edit. On the system canvas an instance node `adaptiveLamp` stands where the region was, with _tiltValue_ on its left, _brightness_ on its right and _AdaptiveLamp · main_ in its body. Two links: `tiltValue` → the required port, the provided port → a top-level `brightness`, which is now an _open_ relationship **bound to** the port (select it: _Takes its value from adaptiveLamp.brightness_). The light is still driven by that `brightness`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/package-as-component.md:32
msgid "**Check.** ⌘2, Step: the same trace, tick for tick. ⌘3: the same placement."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/package-as-component.md:33
msgid "**Look inside.** Double-click the instance. The canvas shows the component's source: `tiltValue` (_requires_), `dimByTilt`, `brightness` (_provides_), laid out as the group was. The bar says _Editing AdaptiveLamp · used by 1 instance_. Change `brightness`'s formula to `dimByTilt(tiltValue) * 2` and _Save definition_; back at _‹ System_, Step again: the light doubled. The source is the one place the behavior is defined."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/package-as-component.md:42
msgid "_The packaging sheet for Adaptive lamp: the computed Requires and Provides, and the decisions only you can make._"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/package-as-component.md:47
msgid "The **boundary** the tool computed for the group became the component's **promise**: crossing-in → _requires_, crossing-out → _provides_, the used domains → _timing parameters_. The tool cannot infer the four decisions above, so it asks."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/package-as-component.md:51
msgid "The top-level design **keeps** a relationship for each provided value (`brightness`), now unresolved and **bound** to the instance's port. A binding is a definition by identity — the top-level `brightness` _is_ the instance's `brightness` — never a copy and never a lookup by name."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/package-as-component.md:55
msgid "Packaging **preserves behavior**: the design computes the same values before and after. (The formal development proves this for the single-domain fragment; Studio's tests check the trace on the wire.)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/package-as-component.md:58
msgid "A member's **identity** survives: `dimByTilt` inside the component is the same relationship, with its place on the canvas."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/package-as-component.md:63
msgid "The menu item is missing on a behavior inside a component's source — packaging there (nested components) is not built; the inspector says so."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/workflows/package-as-component.md:65
msgid "The sheet lists an _Open relationship_ you did not expect — a member has no formula; decide whether it is an input of the component or internal work still to do."
